# AGENTS.md

Operating contract for AI agents working in this repository. Keep this file
short and imperative; link to the detailed documents instead of duplicating
them.

## Mandatory skills (load every session)

> These are hard requirements, not suggestions. Load them before planning or
> editing anything.

| Skill | When | Why |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `self-repair` | Session start, before every task, and immediately after any user correction, preference, or diagnosed bug. | Reads and appends imperative rules in `.agents/autofix.json` so the same mistake is not repeated. |
| `github-triage` | When a bug, regression, or failing test is found, or an issue needs triage or closure. | Deduplicates issues, logs diagnostics, gates closure on tests, and bridges the fix into `autofix.json`. |
| `<stack>-component-dev` | Before creating or editing a component on that stack (e.g. `nextjs-component-dev`, `flutter-component-dev`). | Applies the project's component standards, tokens, tests, and accessibility rules. |
| `<stack>-page-dev` | Before creating or editing a route/page on that stack (e.g. `nextjs-page-dev`). | Applies the page contract: metadata, loading/error boundaries, data fetching, tests. |
| `plan` | Before starting any task that needs three or more steps, and whenever the user says `/plan`. | Interviews first and writes an executable, verifiable plan to `docs/plan/<slug>.md`; planning never edits source files and ends at an approval gate. |

Replace the `<stack>-*` rows with the skills that match this repository's
framework and delete the rows that do not apply. Keep `self-repair`; keep
`github-triage` only when GitHub Issues are enabled for this repository.

## Code search (codebase-memory MCP)

Search code with the `codebase-memory` MCP server, not blind text grep:

- Verify index freshness (`index_status`) or re-index on demand
  (`index_repository`, `mode: "fast"`) — background auto-watch stays off.
- Prefer `search_graph` for symbols, `trace_path` for callers/callees,
  `get_code_snippet` for exact source, and `get_architecture` for orientation.
- Use `search_code` / `rg` / `git grep` only for literal text in Markdown, JSON,
  or CSS, and for line ranges flagged under `parse_partial`.
- When the server is unavailable, fall back to `rg` / `git grep` and say so
  instead of pretending the graph answered.

## Rule capture (`autofix.json`)

- Rules live in `.agents/autofix.json` (fall back to `autofix.json` or
  `.rules/autofix.json` if the project already uses one).
- Newer, higher-numbered rules win over older ones.
- When the user corrects an approach, append the rule immediately — do not
  defer it to the end of the task.
- Keep rules imperative and concrete: `Always …` / `Never … - because …`.

## Planning (`plan` skill)

Any request that needs three or more steps goes through the `plan` skill before
editing: inspect, interview in up to three rounds, then write
`docs/plan/<slug>.md`. Read it before starting such a task, or run `/plan <request>`.

- Planning is read-only. No source file, config, or dependency changes until the
  plan is approved.
- Every step in a plan carries a `Done when` check that a stranger can run.
  "Verify it works" is not a check.
- The plan ends at an approval gate. Producing a plan never authorizes
  implementation, and approving a plan never authorizes a destructive step
  inside it.
- Amend a live plan in place (`A1`, `P2.1a`); never renumber or delete completed
  steps.
- Skip the plan file for one-line fixes — say so instead of adding overhead.

## Engineering standards (`.agents/rules/engineering-standards.md`)

All non-trivial work follows the algorithm-first, functional-programming
baseline installed at `.agents/rules/engineering-standards.md`. Read it before
planning a feature or a fix. The non-negotiables:

- State the algorithm and its edge cases before writing the code.
- Put business logic in pure, deterministic functions; keep I/O, network,
  filesystem access, and state mutation in a thin imperative shell.
- Keep functions small, explicitly typed, and free of hidden dependencies,
  shared mutable state, and mutation of arguments.
- A bug fix ships with a regression test; run the documented test gate and
  report the real command output — never claim a pass without executing it.
- Make the smallest change that satisfies the task: no unrelated refactoring
  and no speculative abstractions.
- Close with the completion report defined in §9 of the standard.

## Code review (`reviewer` subagent)

Before reporting a change as complete, run the read-only `reviewer` subagent on
the diff (`/review`, or "use the reviewer subagent on the current diff"). Review
base branch: `<base>`.

- The reviewer never edits files, runs tests, or writes the report; it returns
  the report in chat.
- Resolve every Blocker, Critical, and High finding before calling the change
  complete. A Medium finding may ship only with its accepted risk recorded in §9
  of the saved report; Low and Nit findings may ship as noted. Re-run the review
  after the fix.
- The reviewer cannot certify the test gate — run it yourself.
- Save the report to `docs/reviews/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` when the change is
  accepted; add `-2`, `-3` for further reviews on the same day. Format:
  `.agents/rules/code-review-report.md`.

## Issue logging (`github-triage`)

Applies when GitHub Issues are enabled (feature F7 of the repository setup).
Every bug, regression, or failing test follows this loop:

1. **Deduplicate first.** Search open issues for the keywords or module before
   creating anything (`gh issue list --search "<keywords>" --state open`). If a
   match exists, comment on it instead of opening a duplicate.
2. **Register.** New issues use `fix(<scope>): <short description>` with labels
   `bug` and `agent-triage`:
   `gh issue create --title "fix(<scope>): …" --label bug --label agent-triage --body "…"`.
3. **Diagnose.** Comment the root cause, affected files, and proposed
   remediation before touching code.
4. **Fix and verify.** Add or update a regression test, then run the project
   gate. Never close an issue while tests fail.
5. **Capture the rule.** Append the preventative rule to `.agents/autofix.json`
   with a sequential `Rule_number`, a valid `Category`, an imperative
   `Instruction`, `Why`, and `"Issue": "#N"`.
6. **Resolve.** Post the summary (changes, test command and result, rule number)
   and close with `state_reason: completed` only after the gate passes.
7. **Report.** Give the user the triage summary: issue link, status, root cause,
   verification, and the rule that was added.

Details and templates live in the `github-triage` skill
(`references/triage-lifecycle.md`, `references/self-repair-bridge.md`,
`resources/templates/diagnostic_comment.md`, `resources/templates/resolution_comment.md`).

## Repository documents

- `PLAN.md` — source of truth for scope, progress, and the continuation
  protocol. Read it top to bottom before starting work.
- `CONTRIBUTING.md` — branch, commit, review, and test-gate rules.
- `SECURITY.md` — how to report vulnerabilities privately.
- `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md` — community expectations.
- `README.md` — setup and run instructions.

## Machine-owned blocks

Framework integrations may inject their own sections into this file (for
example, Next.js writes the `nextjs-agent-rules` block on every `next dev`).
Do not delete or reword those blocks: committing them keeps the working tree
clean, and removing them only recreates the change. Append project rules
outside the managed block.

## Output provenance footer

End **every** response with a short bullet list naming the AI skills and MCP
servers that produced it. This is a standing reporting contract, not a summary
of the work.

- Use exactly this shape:

  ```markdown
  ---
  **Skills & MCP servers used**
  - Skills: `self-repair`, `nextjs-component-dev`
  - MCP servers: `codebase-memory` (`search_code`, `get_code_snippet`), `next-devtools` (`nextjs_index`)
  ```

- List only what actually ran in that turn. Never list a skill or server that was
  merely available or loaded but unused.
- For each MCP server, name the specific tool(s) called in parentheses.
- When nothing was used, write `- Skills: none` / `- MCP servers: none`. Never
  drop the footer.
- Keep it to one line per category. Do not repeat the work summary, findings, or
  next steps in the footer.

## Commit and quality gates

- **Stage, show, wait for approval.** Never run `git commit`, `git commit
  --amend`, or `git push` on your own initiative. Stage only the files that
  belong to the change, show the user `git diff --cached` (stat plus the actual
  hunks) and the proposed Conventional Commit message, then stop and wait. Commit
  only after an explicit approval in the same session.
- **Never sweep in unrelated work.** If other files are already modified, leave
  them unstaged and say so instead of bundling them into the commit.
- Conventional Commits only (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, `ci:`,
  `test:`, `refactor:`); one logical change per commit.
- Run the documented test gate before committing or pushing (for example
  `pnpm lint && pnpm exec tsc --noEmit && pnpm test:all`).
- Never commit secrets, `.env*` values, database dumps, or credentials.
